NTPDATE(8)                     P32W Unix                      NTPDATE(8)

NAME
       ntpdate - set the clock from a time server

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/ntpdate [-q] [server[:port]]

DESCRIPTION
       ntpdate asks a time server for the time by SNTP (UDP port 123):
       the server given, else the first "server NAME" line of
       /etc/ntp.conf, else pool.ntp.org. It tries three times, two
       seconds apart. Half the round trip is added to the server's
       answer.

       As root it sets the clock -- the kernel's, and the battery-backed
       clock's, as date(1) does -- and says how far it moved. With -q it
       only asks, and prints the server's time and the offset.

       /etc/rc runs ntpdate at boot if /etc/ntp.conf exists, so a P32W
       with a network keeps the right time:

              echo server pool.ntp.org > /etc/ntp.conf

FILES
       /etc/ntp.conf

SEE ALSO
       date(1), stime(2)

BUGS
       Whole seconds: the clock keeps no fraction. One answer, from one
       server, is believed; there is no averaging and no slewing.
